<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>PDF math quote render test</title>
    <!-- Claim to be the arXiv PDF, so the client loads that document's PDF-style
         (urn/page-selector) annotations into the sidebar. -->
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.03638" />
  </head>
  <body style="font: 18px/1.7 Georgia, serif; max-width: 40em; margin: 3em auto">
    <h1>PDF math quote render test</h1>
    <p>
      This page carries no annotatable text of its own. It exists to point the client at the
      arXiv PDF above so the sidebar loads that document's PDF-style annotations — the ones
      whose target carries a <code>PageSelector</code> alongside the flattened text-layer
      capture of an equation. The <code>PageSelector</code> is what routes an annotation, at
      intake, to Mathpix OCR of the PDF region rather than to HTML source extraction; h's
      normalization service stores that OCR result as the annotation's
      <code>normalized_quote</code> (<code>method='ocr'</code>). The sidebar displays that
      stored value; it runs no OCR and no recovery of its own when the annotation is shown,
      and it never reads the selector text of a saved annotation.
    </p>

    <p><strong>What to look for.</strong> The sidebar quote should typeset the equation the
      annotation was made over — a real fraction, integral, or operator, matching the region
      on the PDF page the annotation's <code>PageSelector</code> names. The quote will be
      greyed out (redacted styling), because a PDF annotation cannot anchor in this stand-in
      page; that is expected here and is not a normalization failure. The remaining failures
      are distinguishable from each other:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A red normalization-error banner in place of the quote: the API response carried no
        <code>normalized_quote</code>, so there is no <code>annotation_normalized</code> row.</li>
      <li>The garbled text-layer capture shown as the quote: the stored
        <code>normalized_quote</code> is itself a legacy raw row (<code>method</code> of
        <code>raw</code> or <code>identity</code>); run h's normalization reconciliation. The
        sidebar cannot produce this from the selector, which it never reads for a saved
        annotation.</li>
      <li>Literal <code>\(…\)</code> delimiters as plain text: the stored value is LaTeX but
        the sidebar's math rendering did not run over it.</li>
      <li>An equation that typesets but is not the one the annotation covers: intake-time OCR
        read the wrong region of the PDF.</li>
    </ul>
    <script class="js-hypothesis-config" type="application/json">
      {"assetRoot": "http://localhost:3011/hypothesis/1.0.0-dummy-version/", "sidebarAppUrl": "http://localhost:3012/fork-app.html", "apiUrl": "http://localhost:5000/api/", "authDomain": "localhost", "openSidebar": true}
    </script>
    {{{ hypothesisScript }}}
  </body>
</html>
